Cirque Sombre

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + BAILE-FUNK + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Clarinet
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a wedding between two rival circus dynasties.

This sound is the velvet-draped collision of an illicit carnival procession and a seance ritual conducted inside a derelict greenhouse. Think booming, skeletal Baile-Funk rhythms grafted onto melancholic Progressive House synths, then doused in the liquid-dark melancholic cries of clarinets, all under a canopy of chthonic gothic tension. It’s like a spectral ballroom where the clown college dropout is also a necromancer, and the beats are powered by forgotten clockwork mechanisms and desperate whispers.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 120-135 BPM, characterized by a relentless, syncopated march — a percussive foundation that feels both urgent and eerily suspended, often featuring a distinct, irregular low-end throb ("pulse do palhaço").
*   Harmonic Palette: Primarily minor keys (D minor, A minor, E minor) with frequent use of augmented chords and chromaticism, evoking a sense of unease, theatrical grandeur, and a lingering, unresolved tension. The dominant tonal colors are obsidian, deep violet, and tarnished brass.
   Production Signatures: 1) Clarin-FX: heavily processed clarinet lines, from swirling, delayed spectral wails to guttural, distorted growls, acting as both lead and rhythmic percussive element. 2) Ghost-Gating: extreme sidechain compression where atmospheric pad sounds are "sucked" into the kicks, creating a cavernous, breathing effect that amplifies dread. 3) Ritualistic Sample Stitching*: intricate, almost microscopic looping and manipulation of unsettling field recordings—creaking calliopes, muffled distant shouts, the rustle of old velvet, the sound of grinding gears—integrated seamlessly into percussive layers.
*   Dynamics: Tracks often build from sparse, haunting clarinet motifs over a creeping ambient texture, erupting into frenetic, bass-heavy grooves interspersed with moments of sudden, chilling silence or orchestral swells, designed to create an unsettling push-and-pull, like a panic attack at a macabre masquerade.

Scene & Origin Lore

Cirque Sombre first contorted itself into being at the infamous "Vellum & Spoke" nuptials – the shotgun wedding of Penelope Vellum, heiress to the world's most reclusive taxidermy circus, and Barnaby Spoke, the flamboyant, snake-charming scion of a rival family notorious for their "mechanical menagerie" attractions. Held in an abandoned greenhouse ballroom, the music blurred the lines between celebratory fervor and existential dread. Attendees, adorned in decaying velvet, skeletal corsets, and grotesque, custom-fabricated masks fashioned from forgotten carousel parts, swayed to rhythms they barely understood, their bodies involuntarily jerking to the erratic, bass-heavy clarinet cries. It became the soundtrack of the "Veiled Revels," clandestine, pop-up events held in forgotten industrial spaces and overgrown botanical gardens, where the fashion embraced a blend of decaying rococo glamour and industrial grunge, always with an element of performative discomfort – spiked crinolines, stained silks, and perpetually smudged kohl eyes.

Instruments: Clariné-Abyssal (a custom-modified bass clarinet with integrated sub-harmonic exciters and a vocoder modulator), the "Grinder-Engine" Bass Synthesizer (a modular beast designed to emulate rusted machinery and grinding gears), "Perdition-Kit" Drum Machines (heavily sampled and warped traditional percussion alongside glitched 808s and live, overdriven samba bells), the "Spectral Veil" Pad Synthesizer (for dark, evolving atmospheric textures), and occasional "Gutter-Strings" (over-bowed, distorted cello samples).

For Fans Of: Slikback, Gazelle Twin, The Haxan Cloak, Arca

